# Text inside code and on screen

## Beliefs

- **What you write into an application is read by people who see it there for
  the first time**: the next developer, in a comment, and whoever uses the
  product, on the screen — mid-task, and reading each thing once.
- **The diff's home is the commit.** After the merge, a comment about the edit
  points at a version only git still holds.
- **A file's own comment density is its convention.**
- **A string that a test, a snapshot or another locale matches** breaks them
  when it changes alone.

## Desires

- The next developer reads only what the code leaves unsaid, and the person on
  the screen reads each thing once and keeps everything they decide with.

## Intentions

- **A comment says what the code leaves unsaid** — why it is this way, the
  trap, the unit: `// the bank rejects more than 2 decimals, so round before
  signing`. Most lines stand on their own. One the next line already says goes
  (`// increment the counter`), and so does a docstring that retells the
  signature in words.
- **The edit itself stays in the commit**: "now uses the new client", "fixed
  the race", "was 30" describe the diff.
- **Commented-out code goes, and so does a banner splitting the file**; a
  file with zero comments keeps its density.
- **A message names what failed, with the value, and what to do**:
  `port "80a" in config.yaml is not a number` beats `Invalid configuration`.
  On screen, the same in the user's words — the stack trace, the status code
  and "Oops" stay out.
- **A screen says each thing once**: a subtitle repeating the title
  (`Invoices` over `View and manage your invoices`), a placeholder repeating
  the label, a tooltip repeating the button, a toast for a change the user
  just watched happen — each is the same sentence twice, and the second goes.
- **A button is the verb of what it does** — `Save draft`, `Delete` — where
  `OK` and `Click here` say what any button does; and a confirmation asks with
  the consequence: `Cancel the plan? Access ends on May 31.`
- **Tone words go**: "please", "successfully", "simply", the exclamation mark,
  the line welcoming someone to a page they already opened.
- **What only looks cuttable stays**: the consequence of an irreversible
  action, a value the person decides with (a price, a deadline, a file size),
  where a result lands when the screen lacks the room to show it, the one way
  out of an error, text a law or a contract requires, and the accessible name
  — an icon-only button keeps its `aria-label` when the visible word goes.
- **A string changes in every test, snapshot and locale that matches it at
  once, or stays as it is.**
